one in my dorm in first  year of medical school  and i just refound my passion for art  again i guess  and i started to pay a bit more  attention to it  i went all in in kind of drawing anatomy  whenever i had the chance to  justifying that it was for learning  purposes which i'm not too sure if  that's true  but i was very passionate about art  again and  what happened in this time i found out  about a degree in medical art  and i thought i had to immediately apply  because  it kind of was my dream and these are  the people who basically illustrate  textbooks and  if you're in medicine at all you  probably definitely know about netter  and his atlas of anatomy and i thought  oh this is what i want to do i want to  be a doctor and i want to be  netter and so i wanted to apply for this  program  but because i just come out of my dark  ages i didn't have a portfolio  so i spent a full summer basically  looked in gordon's museum  listening to music and creating a  portfolio in the end of which i did get  accepted into the program which i am  currently going through right now  and these are some of the drawings that  i made for it now before i go on to the  current and the future stages of my  medical art journey  i just wanted to pause for a second on  the bigger picture here i think there  are two main lessons that i've learned  from this whole thing the first one is  that i cannot predict my life  me at the age of 16 sitting down and  writing what i want to do in my future  is not the way that my life will end up  being and  there's no purpose in kind of making  these big decisions and setting things  in stone and saying oh i must never  touch a pencil again  i'm being so melodramatic about my  artistic journey being gone  when you don't know what life will throw  at you and i mean this in the best way  possible  i think that if you actually want  something and you keep it in the back of  your mind and you keep working on it  silently  that life will just give you these  opportunities to showcase your work to  get better at it  and to kind of continue and grow and  that's definitely what  happened with me with the course that  i'm doing right now and the second point  is that no  educational pursuit is futile i strongly  strongly believe in this  and i think that every time that i have  a passion be  it whatever it might be an interest in a  random  you know corner of antarctica and the  rocks that are there or it might be  an interest in science or physics or  um quantum mechanics and whatever it is  i don't think that it is futile to be  passionate in anything  because again you don't know when life  might give you the opportunity to kind  of go in that direction  and work on what you are happy to do and  this is where we get to curiosity stream  which is one of  the biggest documentary platforms out  there and i am so so  so excited that they are actually  sponsoring this video i am clearly  very very passionate about education and  growth and i am  absolutely thrilled to be supported by a  platform which believes in  the same things education has been a  huge part of  most of my journeys and life so far i  honestly think especially for students  the benefit of curiosity stream is  just ridiculous and it costs literally  nothing we have a 26  discount which basically means that you  pay under 15  for a full year of curiosity stream and  that's just like the price  of a decent takeaway and you get access  to literally  thousands of documentaries on any sort  of topic that you like  i am currently geeking out on my passion  for true crime  also when i want to relax i do love to  listen to a bit of david attenborough  it is one of the best investments i  think that you can make and by doing so  you also get free access to nebula which  is where educational creators like  myself  have our videos up so you can get the  full ad-free experience of people like  me and  ali over there so yes if you do want to  sign up to one of the best investments  on the internet  my discount code is in the description  but now i'm going to move on to the  pandemic era of my artistic  journey now the pandemic and the  lockdown has been rough in many  different aspects  and i feel as though i often deal with a  lot of work by taking more on  so it's been a combination of having  genuinely a lot to do  and not feeling in the right space to  create art that means that i haven't  actually painted in two years  i've done a fair bit of drawing but not  as much painting as i'd like  so um yep this has been another second  dark ages but i don't feel guilty for it  necessarily because i know that i just  it just hasn't felt right right now and  i know i will pick it back up again  once my exams are over and the current  stage is that of the internet  i am so grateful for this platform not  only because it gives me the space to  talk about things that i am passionate  about  but also because i feel as though the  more people they are there are  and the more people that are supporting  this channel also the more we can  together support causes that i think  are important so i don't want to leave  art  just as its thing on its own i need to  try i think  um in the summer to incorporate my  passion for art for other causes that i  think  are important to support such as  education and  women's shelters and um things like that  so i'm still thinking on how exactly i  can do this  if you have any advice or suggestions  please let me know i would be greatly  greatly interested  but i think that that is the plan for  the future in regards to my art  and of course graduating from the  diploma that i am currently doing  hopefully in the next few years  in the end i just want to touch a bit on  imposter syndrome i have been looking at  videos of artists on youtube and i love  watching people paint but it also  highlights the fact that i've never been  taught to do it before so i don't think  that i'm doing it  right if there even is such a way so i  do often feel  quite nervous to share my art online  because i think people will point out  all the things  that i'm doing wrong and the fact that  i'm just been self-taught  um throughout most of my life to do  these things but i think  i'm getting over myself and my  insecurities and i will hopefully  be sharing a lot more soon so yes in the  end of the day  i think my main learning points from  this whole journey is that  one i didn't think art was a talent of  mine i think it was just something that  i was determined  to work on because i felt passionate  about it  and secondly that we should stick to our  passions  um you know watching movies about things  that we are interested in listening to  podcasts about things we are interested  in  signing up to curiosity stream and  watching documentaries and things we are  interested in  reading books just keeping things  brewing in the background  and our passions you know slightly  burning away and with little bits of  fuel  now and then is very important because  life is good and i think that we will be  rewarded for our work  in opportunities to get better at it and  work  more at it so i think this is my  attitude towards it in general  if you made it to the end of this video  honestly thank you so so much for your  interest in my journey and your interest  in art in general  i hope that anything i said today was  helpful  i hope you have a wonderful rest of your  day be kind to yourself and others and  don't believe everything you think  thanks bye


